awoo! you need the `InnerBlocks` component from the Gutenberg editor to add child containers to your block. it lets you make blocks that hold other blocks, so you can nest them up nicely.

to do that, add `InnerBlocks` to your block's edit function, and set it to allow whatever blocks you want inside. here's an example:

```javascript
import { InnerBlocks } from '@wordpress/block-editor';

function MyBlockEdit( { attributes, setAttributes } ) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>My Block</h2>
      <InnerBlocks
        allowedBlocks={ [ 'core/group' ] }
      />
    </div>
  );
}
```
in this case, `InnerBlocks` is added to `MyBlockEdit`, and it's set to only allow `core/group` blocks as children. that's what the user will see when they try to add stuff inside.

with that setup, the block editor will only let users add `core/group` blocks as child containers, wan~
